// A sea of sand: a height field whose land is written down rather than drawn.
//
// The two islands beside this one are the other two ways to make terrain here -- `island.igl` reads
// its heights from a picture, and `island-from-a-function.igl` marches an isosurface.  This is the
// third: a **function height field**, which is described like the isosurface and meshed like the
// picture.  Nothing sits beside this file; the desert is nine lines of arithmetic.
//
// **The function is evaluated over the unit square**, so `x` and `z` run from 0 to 1 whatever the
// field is later scaled to, and every frequency below is "cycles across the whole desert".
//
// **What it answers is the height outright.**  A height field made from an *image* quietly
// multiplies its heights by a quarter; a function's are taken as they are.  That is the one
// difference between the two forms worth carrying in your head, and it is why this scene needs no
// `scale [1, 0.25, 1]` anywhere.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **The dunes.**  A wave in Z would give parallel ridges and read as corrugated iron -- which is
// exactly how the first draft looked.  Three things fix it, and all three are needed:
//
//   * the phase is **wandered by noise in X**, which bends each crest instead of shifting them all
//     together.  The frequency of that noise matters more than its size: warped at 2 cycles across
//     the desert it merely slides the whole pattern, and at 14 it bends crests over about the
//     distance between them, which is what the eye reads as a dune;
//   * the amplitude is **modulated and clamped to nought**, so ridges rise, run for a while and die
//     away rather than crossing the field from edge to edge;
//   * the dunes ride on a **broad rolling sand sheet**, so the ground beneath them is not a plane.
//
// `pow(..., 3.2)` on the raised sine is what makes a dune shape of it: broad troughs and a narrow
// crest, rather than a sine's symmetrical hump.
//
// **A fine grain was tried and removed.**  A `0.02 * noise(52x, 0, 52z)` ripple sits below a pixel
// at this distance, so it adds no texture and gives the sampler a great deal of aliasing to clean
// up -- the same lesson the water scenes teach about wave trains too small to see.
//
// **`smooth` is why this is 1100 samples and not 2000.**  A height field is built of *flat*
// triangles, so without it a smooth surface shows the edges of them as a fine diamond pattern
// wherever the light rakes across -- and dunes are the worst case there is: smooth, and lit at a
// graze.  Flat, this scene wanted 2000 samples before the hatching went; smooth, 1100 is cleaner
// still and renders in rather less than half the time.
//
// Smoothing costs nothing to compute, and it *only shades*: the shadow edges are real geometry, and
// they are what still wants a grid this fine.  Terrain with crags in it -- the islands -- hides its
// faceting well enough that it never asked for this.
//
// **The sun stands at 20 degrees, and lower is worse.**  At 10 degrees the shadows are longer than
// the gap between dunes, so every trough fills in solid black; at 20 they reach about as far as the
// next dune and the troughs stay legible.  The azimuth is 45 degrees off the way the dunes run:
// straight down them the light is flat, straight across it is a silhouette.
//
// **Rendered with `-a adaptive:1:0.5`**, which is the committed picture; see `gallery/sources.txt`.
context {
    parallel pixel scanner
    angles are degrees
    info {
        title 'Dunes'
        author 'J. Stephen Kress'
        description 'A desert of wind-blown sand, written as nine lines of arithmetic.'
        copyright true
        software __software__
    }
}

camera { location [-165, 170, -250]  look at [55, 0, 95]  field of view 46 }

background physical sky { sun elevation 20  sun azimuth 50  turbidity 3.2  brightness 3.2 }
sky light { samples 28 }

// Sand carries on past the dune field, so its far edge is not the horizon.
plane { material { pigment [0.86, 0.63, 0.36]  specular 0.03  shininess 8 } }

heightfield {
    function {
        // The sand sheet the dunes stand on.
          0.42 * (0.50 * noise(2.2 * x, 0, 2.2 * z)
                + 0.25 * noise(4.6 * x, 0, 4.6 * z)
                + 0.12 * noise(9.4 * x, 0, 9.4 * z))
        // The dunes: a wave across the wind, its crests bent and its ridges fading in and out.
        + 0.58 * pow(0.5 + 0.5 * sin(110 * z
                + 10.0 * noise(14.0 * x, 0, 2.4 * z)
                +  4.0 * noise(30.0 * x, 0, 6.0 * z)), 3.2)
              * clamp(1.8 * noise(9.0 * x, 0, 5.0 * z) - 0.20, 0, 1)
    }
    samples 1100
    smooth
    material { pigment [0.86, 0.63, 0.36]  specular 0.04  shininess 8 }
    translate [-0.5, 0, -0.5]
    scale [1400, 26, 1400]
}
